  "answer": "Alveoli are designed to maximise the exchange of gases due to their unique structure. They are small, numerous air sacs located at the end of the bronchioles in the lungs. The large number of alveoli increases the total surface area available for gas exchange, allowing more oxygen to be absorbed into the blood and more carbon dioxide to be expelled. Additionally, the walls of the alveoli are very thin, which facilitates the diffusion of gases between the air in the alveoli and the blood in the surrounding capillaries. The moist environment inside the alveoli also helps in the efficient exchange of gases.",
